NAME
    Parallel::Goroutine - Go-style goroutines and channels for Perl

VERSION
    0.01

SYNOPSIS
        use Parallel::Goroutine;

        my $jobs    = chan();
        my $results = chan();

        for (1 .. 3) {
            go {
                while (1) {
                    my ($job, $ok) = $jobs->recv;
                    last unless $ok;
                    $results->send($job * 2);
                }
            };
        }

        $jobs->send($_) for 1 .. 10;
        $jobs->close;

        my @answers = map { scalar $results->recv } 1 .. 10;
        wait_all();

DESCRIPTION
    This module brings the two core ideas of Go's concurrency model to
    Perl: goroutines that run independently, and channels that carry values
    between them.

    A goroutine is started with "go". Each goroutine is a separate process
    created with fork, so goroutines run in parallel on all CPU cores.
    Because they are processes, goroutines do not share variables. They
    communicate only by sending values through channels, and every value
    sent through a channel is copied. Hashes, arrays and nested data
    structures can be sent. Code references and filehandles cannot.

    A channel is a pipe with a small protocol on top. It is safe to use
    with several senders and several receivers at the same time. A channel
    holds roughly 64KB of data; when it is full, "send" waits until a
    receiver has made room.

    Because it relies on fork, flock and pipes, this module works on
    Unix-like systems only. Each goroutine is a whole process, so use tens
    or hundreds of goroutines, not thousands.

INSTALLATION
    The usual:

        perl Makefile.PL
        make
        make test
        make install

    This module is not supported on Windows.

DEPENDENCIES
    Exporter, Fcntl, File::Temp, IO::Handle, IO::Select, POSIX, Storable.
    All are core modules.

